# :global and :vglobal typed from normal mode (repeat.txt *:g*).

=== :g/pat/d deletes every matching line
ref:    repeat.txt *:g*
quote:  Execute the Ex command [cmd] (default ":p") on the lines within [range] where {pattern} matches.
given:
  |x one
  two
  x three
  four
  x five
keys:   :g/x/d<CR>
expect:
  two
  |four
mode:   normal

=== :g/pat/d reports the deleted lines
ref:    options.txt *'report'*
quote:  When the number of changed lines is more than 'report' a message will be given for most ":" commands.
given:
  |x one
  two
  x three
  x four
keys:   :g/x/d<CR>
expect: |two
msg:    3 fewer lines
mode:   normal

=== :g is undone in one step
ref:    repeat.txt *E147*
quote:  The undo/redo command will undo/redo the whole global command at once.
given:
  |x one
  two
  x three
keys:   :g/x/d<CR>
expect: |two
undo:
  |x one
  two
  x three
mode:   normal

=== :g/pat/d leaves the deleted register holding the last line
ref:    repeat.txt *:v*
quote:  In a second scan the [cmd] is executed for each marked line, as if the cursor was in that line.
given:
  |x one
  two
  x three
keys:   :g/x/d<CR>
expect: |two
regs:   "" = "x three\n"   "1 = "x three\n"   "2 = "x one\n"
mode:   normal

=== :g/pat/d _ leaves the registers alone
ref:    repeat.txt *:v*
quote:  Using the underscore after `:d` avoids clobbering registers or the clipboard.
given:
  |x one
  two
  x three
keys:   :g/x/d _<CR>
expect: |two
regs:   "" = (unset)   "1 = (unset)
mode:   normal

=== :g! runs on the lines that don't match
ref:    repeat.txt *:g*
quote:  Execute the Ex command [cmd] (default ":p") on the lines within [range] where {pattern} does NOT match.
given:
  |x one
  two
  x three
  four
keys:   :g!/x/d<CR>
expect:
  x one
  |x three
mode:   normal

=== :v is the same as :g!
ref:    repeat.txt *:v*
quote:  Same as :g!.
given:
  |x one
  two
  x three
  four
keys:   :v/x/d<CR>
expect:
  x one
  |x three
mode:   normal

=== :g with a range only looks at those lines
ref:    repeat.txt *:v*
quote:  The default for [range] is the whole buffer (1,$).
given:
  |x
  x
  x
  x
keys:   :2,3g/x/s/x/y/<CR>
expect:
  x
  y
  |y
  x
mode:   normal

=== :g with another delimiter
ref:    repeat.txt *:v*
quote:  Instead of the '/' which surrounds the {pattern}, you can use any other single byte character, but not an alphabetic character
given:
  |a/b
  c
  d/e
keys:   :g#/#d<CR>
expect: |c
mode:   normal

=== :g without a match gives a plain message
ref:    pattern.txt *E486*
quote:  Note that for the `:global` command, when used in legacy script, you get a normal message "Pattern not found", for Vi compatibility.
given:
  |one
  two
keys:   :g/zzz/d<CR>
expect:
  |one
  two
msg:    "Pattern not found: zzz"
mode:   normal

=== :v matching every line gives a message
ref:    pattern.txt *E538*
quote:  when the pattern matches in every line with `:vglobal`.
given:
  |one
  two
keys:   :v/o/d<CR>
expect:
  |one
  two
msg:    "Pattern found in every line: o"
mode:   normal

=== :g/^/m0 reverses the buffer
ref:    usr_12.txt *12.4*
quote:  The |:global| command can be combined with the |:move| command to move all the lines before the first line, resulting in a reversed file.
given:
  |one
  two
  three
  four
keys:   :g/^/m0<CR>
expect:
  |four
  three
  two
  one
undo:
  one
  |two
  three
  four
mode:   normal

=== :g/pat/m$ moves matching lines to the end
ref:    change.txt *:m*
quote:  Move the lines given by [range] to below the line given by {address}.
given:
  |x one
  two
  x three
  four
keys:   :g/x/m$<CR>
expect:
  two
  four
  x one
  |x three
mode:   normal

=== :g/pat/t. duplicates matching lines
ref:    change.txt *:co*
quote:  Copy the lines given by [range] to below the line given by {address}.
given:
  |x one
  two
  x three
keys:   :g/x/t.<CR>
expect:
  x one
  x one
  two
  x three
  |x three
mode:   normal

=== :g/pat/t$ copies matching lines to the end
ref:    change.txt *:co*
quote:  Copy the lines given by [range] to below the line given by {address}.
given:
  |x one
  two
  x three
keys:   :g/x/t$<CR>
expect:
  x one
  two
  x three
  x one
  |x three
mode:   normal

=== :g/pat/s substitutes in matching lines
ref:    repeat.txt *E147*
quote:  Any Ex command can be used, see |ex-cmd-index|.
given:
  |x a
  a
  x a
keys:   :g/x/s/a/b/<CR>
expect:
  x b
  a
  |x b
mode:   normal

=== :g/pat/s//new/ reuses the global pattern
ref:    repeat.txt *E147*
quote:  The global command sets both the last used search pattern and the last used substitute pattern (this is vi compatible).
given:
  |pat pat
  other
  pat
keys:   :g/pat/s//PAT/g<CR>
expect:
  PAT PAT
  other
  |PAT
mode:   normal

=== :g sets the last search pattern
ref:    repeat.txt *E147*
quote:  The global command sets both the last used search pattern and the last used substitute pattern (this is vi compatible).
given:
  |one
  foo
  three
  foo
keys:   :g/foo/normal A!<CR>ggn
expect:
  one
  |foo!
  three
  foo!
mode:   normal

=== :g/pat/normal runs normal-mode keys on each line
ref:    repeat.txt *E147*
quote:  To execute a Normal mode command, you can use the `:normal` command
given:
  |x one
  two
  x three
keys:   :g/x/normal A;<CR>
expect:
  x one;
  two
  x three|;
mode:   normal

=== :g/pat/normal dw
ref:    repeat.txt *E147*
quote:  To execute a Normal mode command, you can use the `:normal` command
given:
  |x one
  two
  x three
keys:   :g/x/norm dw<CR>
expect:
  one
  two
  |three
mode:   normal

=== :g/pat/j joins each match with the next line
ref:    change.txt *:j*
quote:  The default behavior is to join the current line with the line below it.
given:
  |key:
  a
  key:
  b
keys:   :g/key/j<CR>
expect:
  key: a
  |key: b
mode:   normal

=== :g with a relative range in the command
ref:    repeat.txt *:v*
quote:  NOTE [cmd] may contain a range
given:
  |a
  x
  b
  x
keys:   :g/x/-1d<CR>
expect:
  x
  |x
mode:   normal

=== :g/pat/.,+1d loses the marks of deleted lines
ref:    repeat.txt *:v*
quote:  If a line is deleted its mark disappears.
given:
  |x1
  x2
  b
  x3
  c
keys:   :g/x/.,+1d<CR>
expect: |b
mode:   normal

=== :g/pat/+1d deletes the line after each match
ref:    repeat.txt *:v*
quote:  NOTE [cmd] may contain a range
given:
  |head
  one
  head
  two
keys:   :g/head/+1d<CR>
expect:
  head
  |head
mode:   normal

=== :g nested with :v
ref:    repeat.txt *E147*
quote:  This is useful to find all lines that match a pattern and do not match another pattern
given:
  |found one
  found notfound
  other
  found two
keys:   :g/found/v/notfound/d<CR>
expect:
  found notfound
  |other
mode:   normal

=== :g/^$/d deletes the empty lines
ref:    repeat.txt *:g*
quote:  Execute the Ex command [cmd] (default ":p") on the lines within [range] where {pattern} matches.
given:
  |one

  two

keys:   :g/^$/d<CR>
expect:
  one
  |two
mode:   normal

=== :g/pat/y A appends matching lines to an emptied register
ref:    change.txt *:y*
quote:  Yank [range] lines [into register x].
given:
  |x one
  two
  x three
keys:   qaq:g/x/y A<CR>
expect:
  x one
  two
  |x three
regs:   "a = "\nx one\nx three\n"
mode:   normal

=== :g/pat/> shifts matching lines
ref:    change.txt *:>*
quote:  Shift [range] lines one 'shiftwidth' right.
given:
  |x one
  two
  x three
keys:   :set sw=2 et<CR>:g/x/><CR>
expect:
    x one
  two
    |x three
mode:   normal

=== :g/pat/s with a pattern that fails on some lines
ref:    repeat.txt *:v*
quote:  If an error message is given for a line, the command for that line is aborted and the global command continues with the next marked or unmarked line.
given:
  |x a
  x
  x a
keys:   :g/x/s/a/b/<CR>
expect:
  x b
  x
  |x b
mode:   normal
